1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,697
Watt's steam engine saved coal, and Britain

2
00:00:02,797 --> 00:00:05,169
burned more. Cheaper AI does the same.

3
00:00:05,483 --> 00:00:09,258
In 1769, James Watt patented a separate condenser, so

4
00:00:09,358 --> 00:00:12,402
his engines needed less than half the coal.

5
00:00:12,716 --> 00:00:16,230
Each job cost less coal. So mines, mills and trains

6
00:00:16,330 --> 00:00:20,339
added engines. Britain's coal output grew over four times.

7
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:23,392
Economist William Jevons later described it:

8
00:00:23,492 --> 00:00:25,200
cheaper use brings more use.

9
00:00:25,516 --> 00:00:29,291
AI sells text in tokens, small word pieces. In two years,

10
00:00:29,391 --> 00:00:33,438
their price at one quality level fell over two hundred times.

11
00:00:33,750 --> 00:00:37,005
Each answer costs less. So apps and agents add AI.

12
00:00:37,105 --> 00:00:40,562
Google's monthly tokens grew fifty times in one year.

13
00:00:40,866 --> 00:00:44,629
Now AI feeds coal. Coal makes about 30 percent of data

14
00:00:44,729 --> 00:00:49,065
center power, and some US utilities delay coal plant closures.

15
00:00:49,366 --> 00:00:53,533
Experts expect data centers to use twice as much power this decade.

16
00:00:53,850 --> 00:00:57,888
Each job became cheaper, and the total grew. Same trick, two worlds.

